"papers":1,"designs":{"animal":1},"evidence_level":"animal","eco":{"id":"ECO:0007120","label":"animal model system study evidence"},"oxford":{"level":"5","label":"bench or animal research"},"evidence_lines":[{"design":"animal","eco":{"id":"ECO:0007120","label":"animal model system study evidence"},"mesh_publication_types":[],"papers":1,"pmids":["39317688"]}],"pmids":["39317688"]},{"id":"cognition","label":"Focus and memory","papers":1,"designs":{"review":1},"evidence_level":"review_only","eco":{"id":"ECO:0000033","label":"author statement supported by traceable reference"},"oxford":{"level":"5","label":"expert review without primary human data"},"evidence_lines":[{"design":"review","eco":{"id":"ECO:0000033","label":"author statement supported by traceable reference"},"mesh_publication_types":["Review"],"papers":1,"pmids":["32235333"]}],"pmids":["32235333"]}],"years":{"from":1960,"to":2026},"basis":"MeSH indexing and publication types of the most relevant PubMed records; counts are of papers in the sample, not findings.","vocabulary":{"evidence_type":"ECO (Evidence & Conclusion Ontology)","study_design":"MeSH Publication Types","strength":"Oxford CEBM 2009 levels","structure":"SEPIO assertion / evidence line / evidence item"},"search_url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/?term=Camphene%5BAll%20Fields%5D"},"trials":{"total":0,"matched_loosely":0,"studies":[],"source":"ClinicalTrials.gov","search_url":"https://clinicaltrials.gov/search?intr=Camphene"},"is_terpenoid":true,"consumer_summary":{"model":"gemini-3.1-pro-preview","generated":"2026-09-11","what_it_is":"Camphene is a bicyclic monoterpene, a volatile aromatic molecule that helps give many essential oils their characteristic scent. As a widespread plant metabolite and natural fragrance, it contributes to the pungent, earthy, and herbal aromas of the plants that produce it. You encounter it frequently in daily life whenever you smell fresh herbs, citrus rinds, or pine needles.","found_in":"It is naturally abundant in familiar culinary and medicinal plants, including sage (Salvia officinalis), rosemary (Rosmarinus officinalis), ginger (Zingiber officinale), and lemon (Citrus limon). It is also found in the essential oils of valerian root and spruce trees.","why_researchers_care":"Researchers are drawn to camphene for its broad presence in traditional botanical extracts and its potential as a natural protective agent. Much of the scientific interest focuses on its ability to interact with microbes and oxidative stress, alongside its structural relationship to other well-known terpenes like camphor and limonene. The strongest evidence exploring its effects currently comes from human observational studies (Oxford level 2b), which are mapping how this common dietary and environmental compound interacts with human biology.","structure_function":[{"claim_id":"antimicrobial","statement":"Camphene is being studied for its potential to support natural defenses against microbial activity.","evidence_level":"human_observational","basis":"This rests on 26 papers, including 7 human observational studies and several in vitro tests.","eco":{"id":"ECO:0000180","label":"clinical study evidence"},"oxford":{"level":"2b","label":"individual cohort or observational study"},"evidence_lines":[{"design":"human_observational","eco":"ECO:0000180","papers":7},{"design":"animal","eco":"ECO:0007120","papers":5},{"design":"in_vitro","eco":"ECO:0000181","papers":7}],"paper_count":26},{"claim_id":"antioxidant","statement":"Early work points toward camphene having properties that may support the body's management of oxidative stress.","evidence_level":"human_observational","basis":"This is based on 16 papers, primarily animal and in vitro research, alongside one human observational study.","eco":{"id":"ECO:0000180","label":"clinical study evidence"},"oxford":{"level":"2b","label":"individual cohort or observational study"},"evidence_lines":[{"design":"human_observational","eco":"ECO:0000180","papers":1},{"design":"animal","eco":"ECO:0007120","papers":5},{"design":"in_vitro","eco":"ECO:0000181","papers":3}],"paper_count":16},{"claim_id":"insect","statement":"Camphene is being explored for its potential to act as a natural botanical insect repellent.","evidence_level":"animal","basis":"This observation is currently supported by 5 animal and bench studies.","eco":{"id":"ECO:0007120","label":"animal model system study evidence"},"oxford":{"level":"5","label":"bench or animal research"},"evidence_lines":[{"design":"animal","eco":"ECO:0007120","papers":5}],"paper_count":5},{"claim_id":"anxiety","statement":"Early animal work points toward a potential role for camphene in supporting calm and managing anxiety-like behaviors.","evidence_level":"animal","basis":"This is based on a small pool of research, including one animal study and one expert review.","eco":{"id":"ECO:0007120","label":"animal model system study evidence"},"oxford":{"level":"5","label":"bench or animal research"},"evidence_lines":[{"design":"animal","eco":"ECO:0007120","papers":1},{"design":"review","eco":"ECO:0000033","papers":1}],"paper_count":2},{"claim_id":"neuroprotection","statement":"Researchers are investigating whether camphene may support long-term neurological health and cellular protection.","evidence_level":"human_observational","basis":"This rests on early evidence from one human observational study and one expert review.","eco":{"id":"ECO:0000180","label":"clinical study evidence"},"oxford":{"level":"2b","label":"individual cohort or observational study"},"evidence_lines":[{"design":"human_observational","eco":"ECO:0000180","papers":1},{"design":"review","eco":"ECO:0000033","papers":1}],"paper_count":2}],"hypothesized_benefits":[{"claim_id":"cancer","area":"Cellular health and oncology","hypothesis":"Researchers are exploring whether camphene exposure or intake is associated with protective effects on cellular health and abnormal cell proliferation. If this bears out, it could highlight specific terpene-rich diets as supportive factors in long-term cellular maintenance.","evidence_level":"human_observational","paper_count":10,"study_to_test_it":"A randomized, placebo-controlled human trial (Oxford level 1b) supplementing a standardized camphene extract alongside standard care, measuring specific biomarkers of cellular proliferation and oxidative stress over six months.","eco":{"id":"ECO:0000180","label":"clinical study evidence"},"oxford":{"level":"2b","label":"individual cohort or observational study"},"evidence_lines":[{"design":"human_observational","eco":"ECO:0000180","papers":8}]},{"claim_id":"antimicrobial","area":"Antimicrobial action","hypothesis":"Scientists are investigating whether camphene can actively inhibit the growth of specific bacteria, such as Escherichia coli. If confirmed, it could lead to new natural preservatives or complementary topical agents.","evidence_level":"human_observational","paper_count":26,"study_to_test_it":"A randomized, double-blind clinical trial (Oxford level 1b) testing a topical camphene formulation against a placebo on localized, superficial bacterial skin colonization, measuring bacterial load reduction over 14 days.","eco":{"id":"ECO:0000180","label":"clinical study evidence"},"oxford":{"level":"2b","label":"individual cohort or observational study"},"evidence_lines":[{"design":"human_observational","eco":"ECO:0000180","papers":7},{"design":"animal","eco":"ECO:0007120","papers":5},{"design":"in_vitro","eco":"ECO:0000181","papers":7}]},{"claim_id":"anti-inflammatory","area":"Inflammatory response","hypothesis":"Early animal work points toward camphene potentially modulating inflammatory pathways. If this translates to humans, it could help explain the soothing properties of traditional herbs like ginger and rosemary.","evidence_level":"animal","paper_count":1,"study_to_test_it":"A human observational cohort study (Oxford level 2b) tracking dietary intake of camphene-rich foods and correlating it with baseline serum inflammatory markers like CRP over a one-year period.","eco":{"id":"ECO:0007120","label":"animal model system study evidence"},"oxford":{"level":"5","label":"bench or animal research"},"evidence_lines":[{"design":"animal","eco":"ECO:0007120","papers":1}]},{"claim_id":"cognition","area":"Focus and memory","hypothesis":"Researchers are curious if the volatile compounds in herbs like sage and rosemary, including camphene, might support cognitive function and memory retention.","evidence_level":"review_only","paper_count":1,"study_to_test_it":"An in vivo animal study (Oxford level 5) administering controlled doses of inhaled camphene to a rodent model, measuring performance in spatial memory tasks like the Morris water maze over four weeks.","eco":{"id":"ECO:0000033","label":"author statement supported by traceable reference"},"oxford":{"level":"5","label":"expert review without primary human data"},"evidence_lines":[{"design":"review","eco":"ECO:0000033","papers":1}]}],"associated_conditions":[{"condition":"metabolic dysfunction-associated steatotic liver disease","mondo_id":"MONDO:0013209","relationship":"Detected in metabolomic studies of the condition."}],"cautions":null,"evidence_at_a_glance":"Best evidence so far is human observational work (Oxford level 2b) exploring its antimicrobial and antioxidant associations; much of the rest is foundational animal and lab research."}}
